# ─── TournaTrak environment configuration ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# YOU DO NOT NEED THIS FILE. A bare `git clone` + `docker compose up -d --build`
# is a complete, secure install:
#
#   • the database password and session secret are GENERATED on first boot and
#     kept in ./secrets — there is nothing to look up and nothing to paste
#   • no admin account exists yet, so /admin shows a "Create Admin Account"
#     form instead of a login. You pick the username and password there, in the
#     browser. There is no default login and no generated password to find.
#
# So this file is a reference for OVERRIDES, and every line below is commented
# out showing the value you get if you leave it alone. Copying it to .env
# verbatim changes nothing — uncomment only what you actually want to change.
#
# NOTE: Compose treats a single "$" here as a variable reference. Any literal
# "$" in a value (a bcrypt hash, a password) must be doubled to "$$".
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ─── Host wiring ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The two settings almost everyone touches.

# Host port for the public site — the only port the stack publishes. Set it
# HERE rather than editing compose.yml: a local edit to a tracked file conflicts
# on every `git pull`, which is how a deploy box ends up unable to update.
# Running a second isolated instance is just another clone with a different
# TT_PORT.
#TT_PORT=3019

# Host user/group the API container runs as, so anything it writes to the
# bind-mounted ./files is owned by you rather than root. Find yours with
# `id -u` and `id -g`. Only matters if your account isn't 1000:1000.
#PUID=1000
#PGID=1000


# ─── Generated secrets ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both are created on first boot if unset and stored in ./secrets. Set them
# only if you need a specific value (restoring a backup, or matching an
# existing database). An explicit value here always wins over a generated one.

# Signs session cookies. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#SESSION_SECRET=

# Password for the bundled Postgres service. Passed to both containers as
# discrete PG* variables rather than a URL, so any characters are safe — no
# encoding involved. The database is never exposed outside the compose network.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# WARNING: Postgres bakes this into ./pgdata on FIRST start. Changing it later
# means restoring the old value or deleting ./pgdata and ./secrets together,
# which destroys the tournament. (`docker compose down -v` no longer does that
# — the database is a plain host directory, not a named volume — so deleting
# the directory is the only way to lose it.)
#POSTGRES_PASSWORD=


# ─── Skipping the browser setup form (automated installs only) ────────────────
# Set ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH to bootstrap an admin account at first boot instead
# of creating one through the browser. Useful when a machine is provisioned by
# a script and nobody will be sitting in front of it.
#
# Leave it unset for a normal install. Don't paste a hash you found in a public
# repository — that is a published password, and this file's history contains
# one for exactly that reason.
#
# Generate your own:
#   docker compose run --rm api node generate-hash.js 'yourpassword'
# then double every "$" in the output (see the Compose note at the top).
#
#ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
#ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
# Require a password change at first login. Defaults to false; the entrypoint
# forces it to true when it bootstraps from a hash left over by an older
# install, so an upgraded box never keeps an unchanged bootstrap password.
#FORCE_PASSWORD_CHANGE=false


# ─── Everything else ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Set true ONLY if the browser speaks HTTPS all the way to this server. Leave
# it false when TLS is terminated upstream (HAProxy, Cloudflare, a reverse
# proxy): the session cookie would carry the Secure flag and the browser would
# then never send it over the plain internal hop, so nobody could log in.
#COOKIE_SECURE=false

# Read by Express itself, not by this app's code — 'production' suppresses
# verbose error stacks in HTTP responses. The API image sets this, so you only
# need it here to override (e.g. 'development' while debugging).
#NODE_ENV=production
